TypeScript is a statically-typed superset of JavaScript that adds type annotations and interfaces to the language. Duck typing, on the other hand, is a concept in object-oriented programming that refers to the practice of determining an object's type based on its behavior rather than its declared type.

In TypeScript, type annotations and interfaces are used to define the shape and behavior of objects, enabling developers to write more robust and maintainable code. For example, a developer can define an interface for a "Person" object like this:

```typescript
interface Person {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  greet(): void;
}
```

This interface specifies that a "Person" object should have a "name" property of type "string", an "age" property of type "number", and a "greet" method that returns "void". When creating instances of this interface, TypeScript will enforce these type constraints, helping to catch errors and maintain consistency.

Duck typing, on the other hand, is a more dynamic approach to object typing. In this paradigm, an object's type is determined by its capabilities, rather than its declared type. This means that if an object has the same behavior as an expected type, it can be treated as if it were of that type, regardless of whether or not it actually implements that type.

In TypeScript, you can leverage duck typing by creating interfaces or types that define the expected behavior of an object, and then using those interfaces or types to type-check variables or parameters. For example:

```typescript
interface Printable {
  print(): void;
}

function printSomething(thing: Printable): void {
  thing.print();
}

// Example usage
class Book implements Printable {
  title: string;
  constructor(title: string) {
    this.title = title;
  }
  print(): void {
    console.log(`Title: ${this.title}`);
  }
}

const myBook = new Book("The Great Gatsby");
printSomething(myBook);
```

In this example, the `Printable` interface defines the expected behavior of objects that can be "printed". The `printSomething` function takes a parameter of type `Printable`, which means that any object that implements the `Printable` interface can be passed as an argument. The `Book` class implements the `Printable` interface, so it can be used as an argument to `printSomething`.

While TypeScript provides a strong typing system, it also supports duck typing to some extent, allowing developers to leverage the benefits of both approaches. However, TypeScript's static typing system provides additional safety and clarity compared to pure duck typing, as it enforces type constraints at compile-time rather than relying solely on runtime behavior.

To create a Discord bot with a single "ban" command using Discord.js v14, follow these steps:

1. Create a new Discord bot application and obtain your bot's token and guild ID (server ID) from the Discord Developer Portal.

2. Install Discord.js v14 by running the following command in your terminal:

   ```bash
   npm install discord.js @discordjs/rest
   ```

3. Create a new JavaScript file and add the following code to initialize your Discord client:

   ```javascript
   const { Client, GatewayIntentBits } = require('discord.js');
   const { Routes } = require('@discordjs/rest');

   const client = new Client({
     intents: [
       GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
       GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
     ],
   });

   const token = 'YOUR_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN_HERE';

   client.login(token);
   ```

   Replace `'YOUR_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN_HERE'` with your Discord bot's token.

4. Create a new file named `commands.js` and add the following code to define your "ban" command:

   ```javascript
   const { SlashCommandBuilder } = require('discord.js');

   module.exports = {
     data: new SlashCommandBuilder()
       .setName('ban')
       .setDescription('Bans the specified user from the server.')
       .addUserOption(option =>
         option
           .setName('user')
           .setDescription('The user to ban.')
           .setRequired(true),
       ),
     async execute(interaction) {
       const user = interaction.options.getUser('user');

       // Your ban implementation here

       await interaction.reply(`Banned ${user.tag}`);
     },
   };
   ```

5. In the same directory, create a new file named `index.js` and add the following code to register your command:

   ```javascript
   const { REST } = require('@discordjs/rest');
   const { Routes } = require('@discordjs/rest');
   const fs = require('fs');

   const commands = [];
   const commandFiles = fs.readdirSync('./commands').filter(file => file.endsWith('.js'));

   for (const file of commandFiles) {
     const command = require(`./commands/${file}`);
     commands.push(command.data);
   }

   const rest = new REST({ version: '10' }).setToken(process.env.TOKEN);

   async function registerCommands() {
     try {
       await rest.put(
         Routes.applicationGuildCommands(process.env.CLIENT_ID, process.env.GUILD_ID),
         { body: commands },
       );

       console.log('Successfully registered application commands.');
     } catch (error) {
       console.error(error);
     }
   }

   registerCommands();
   ```

   Replace `'YOUR_CLIENT_ID_HERE'` and `'YOUR_GUILD_ID_HERE'` with your Discord bot's client ID and the guild ID (server ID) respectively. Make sure to also replace `process.env.TOKEN` with your Discord bot's token.

6. Run `node index.js` to register your command.

7. To test your "ban" command, add a new Slash Command to your Discord server from the application's dashboard or use the following code to add it programmatically:

   ```javascript
   const { REST } = require('@discordjs/rest');
   const { Routes } = require('@discordjs/rest');
   const fs = require('fs');

   const commands = [
     {
       name: 'ban',
       description: 'Bans the specified user from the server.',
       options: [
         {
           name: 'user',
           description: 'The user to ban.',
           type: 6,
           required: true,
         },
       ],
     },
   ];

   const rest = new REST({ version: '10' }).setToken(process.env.TOKEN);

   async function registerCommands() {
     try {
       await rest.put(
         Routes.applicationGuildCommands(process.env.CLIENT_ID, process.env.GUILD_ID),
         { body: commands },
       );

       console.log('Successfully registered application commands.');
     } catch (error) {
       console.error(error);
     }
   }

   registerCommands();
   ```

   Again, replace `'YOUR_CLIENT_ID_HERE'` and `'YOUR_GUILD_ID_HERE'` with your Discord bot's client ID and the guild ID (server ID) respectively. Make sure to also replace `process.env.TOKEN` with your Discord bot's token.

   This will add your "ban" command to your Discord server.

8. Finally, implement your ban logic. Here's an example using the `guildMembers` cache:

   ```javascript
   const { GuildMember, Permissions } = require('discord.js');

   module.exports = {
     data: new SlashCommandBuilder()
       .setName('ban')
       .setDescription('Bans the specified user from the server.')
       .addUserOption(option =>
         option
           .setName('user')
           .setDescription('The user to ban.')
           .setRequired(true),
       ),
     async execute(interaction) {
       const user = interaction.options.getUser('user');

       const member = interaction.guild.members.cache.get(user.id);

       if (!member) {
         return interaction.reply(`Could not find ${user.tag} in this server.`);
       }

       if (
         !interaction.member.permissions.has(Permissions.FLAGS.BAN_MEMBERS) ||
         !interaction.guild.permissionsFor(member).has(Permissions.FLAGS.BAN_MEMBERS)
       ) {
         return interaction.reply('You do not have permission to ban users.');
       }

       await interaction.guild.bans.create(member.user);

       await interaction.reply(`Banned ${user.tag}`);
     },
   };
   ```

   This code will ban the specified user if the bot and the user have the `BAN_MEMBERS` permission. If either the bot or the user lacks permission, the bot will reply with an error message.

Here's a possible implementation of a C++ function that accepts a reference to a std::string containing text in a formatted string and returns a std::string containing the same text in HTML format:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string convertToHtml(const std::string& inputStr) {
    std::string htmlStr;
    std::stringstream ss;

    // Iterate over each character in the input string
    for (const auto c : inputStr) {
        switch (c) {
            case '_':
                ss << "<sub>"; // Subscript
                break;
            case '^':
                ss << "<sup>"; // Superscript
                break;
            case '{':
                ss << "<span style='font-size: "; // Custom font size
                break;
            case '}':
                ss << "px'>"; // Close the span element
                break;
            case '[':
                ss << "<span style='color: "; // Custom font color
                break;
            case ']':
                ss << "'>"; // Close the span element
                break;
            default:
                ss << c; // Leave other characters as is
                break;
        }
    }

    // Close any open HTML tags
    while (!ss.eof()) {
        switch (ss.peek()) {
            case '_':
                ss << "</sub>";
                break;
            case '^':
                ss << "</sup>";
                break;
            case '{':
                ss << "</span>";
                break;
            case '[':
                ss << "</span>";
                break;
            default:
                break;
        }
        ss.ignore(1); // Consume the character
    }

    htmlStr = ss.str();

    return htmlStr;
}
```

This implementation handles four common formatting styles:

1. Subscripts: Enclosed by underscores (`_`) and converted to `<sub>` HTML tags.
2. Superscripts: Enclosed by carats (`^`) and converted to `<sup>` HTML tags.
3. Custom font size: Enclosed by curly braces (`{}`) and converted to a `<span>` HTML tag with a `font-size` style attribute.
4. Custom font color: Enclosed by square brackets (`[]`) and converted to a `<span>` HTML tag with a `color` style attribute.

Note that this implementation assumes that the input string contains only valid characters for the specified formatting styles, and does not perform any error checking. Also, feel free to modify or add more formatting styles as needed.

